  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-12T08:08:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "d2efb5c2-c3b4-4cf8-8624-04ae3c71ceb1",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 120808\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n108 AM PDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible\n  across the region today, with precipitation chances lingering \n  into Tuesday for Lincoln, Clark, and Mohave Counties. \n\n* Breezy conditions and cooler than normal temperatures will\n  continue through the week, with the first freeze of the season\n  possible for portions of the Owens Valley and southwestern Great\n  Basin.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next weekend.\n\nLate night radar and satellite imagery show ongoing showers across \nClark, Lincoln, and Mohave Counties as an upper-level trough pushes \ninto the area. This trough will continue its slow trek east today, \nwith isolated-to-scattered showers and thunderstorms expected across \nthe region today as a result. Moisture across the region will not be \nas abundant as it was on Sunday when Rachel's remnants moved \nthrough, so widespread flash flooding is not expected to pose as \ngreat of a threat today. However, isolated instances of flash \nflooding will be possible with stronger storms that develop. In \naddition to isolated instances of flash flooding, lightning, gusty \nthunderstorm winds, and localized moderate-to-heavy rain will be \npossible with storms today. The upper-level trough will continue to \ndig through the region on Tuesday, with around a 20 to 50 percent \nchance for isolated showers and thunderstorms across the Arizona \nStrip, northeastern Clark County, and eastern Lincoln County on \nTuesday afternoon. By Wednesday, precipitation chances will have \ndisappeared from the area as the trough shifts off to our east. \n\nIn addition to showers and thunderstorms, this trough will also \nbreezy conditions to southern Nevada, northwestern Arizona, and \nsoutheastern California. Breezy southerly winds are expected today \nwith peak gusts in the 25 to 40 mph range this afternoon. Winds will \nshift to the northwest on Tuesday as a cold front swings through the \narea, with breezier northerly winds lingering through the work week. \nAs the cold front moves through, some of the cities/towns in the \nnorthwestern portion of our forecast area will have a shot at their \nfirst freeze of the season. Currently, the NBM boasts an 80 to 95% \nchance of freezing temperatures on Tuesday morning for the Bishop \nand Dyer areas, where a Freeze Watch remains in effect. Looking at \nthe rest of the region, temperatures on Tuesday will run around 15 \nto 20 degrees cooler than normal. Beyond Tuesday, temperatures will \ngradually rebound during the second half of the week with \ntemperatures approaching normal over the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Non-\nimpactful showers linger in the valley, mostly to the south of the \nterminal. Breezy south winds linger into the late night before gusts \ncut off and prevailing winds reduce. Additional shower activity \narrives in the early to mid morning, but does not look impactful. \nWinds turn south-southwest into Monday afternoon and become gusty \nwith peak gusts around 25 kts. Shower activity exits into the early \nnight with winds becoming southwest and light.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Shower activity is \nreducing and winds are starting to decrease. Winds across the region \nbecome light in the late night. Light winds last through much of the \nmorning but breezy to gusty  south to southwest winds build in the \nlate morning and into the afternoon with spotty shower activity. \nShowers exit into Monday evening with winds at most sites losing \ntheir gusts and becoming light. KDAG, KBIH, and KIFP see some \nreductions in wind, but remain breezy beyond the end of the TAF \nperiod.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
